sdio: fix double-free when sdio_artosyn_probe() fails

sdio_artosyn_probe() calls sdio_set_drvdata(func, dev) unconditionally
near the top, well before any of its failure paths, but the "fail:"
cleanup at the bottom never clears that back to NULL before kfree()ing
dev. Before 0005-dnld-retry-firmware-chunks-instead-of-faking-success
existed, oal_add_card() failing this way was effectively unreachable (a
failed firmware chunk was silently treated as success instead, so probe
always "succeeded"), so this path was never really exercised. With that
patch correctly propagating a real download failure, this became
reachable, and the kernel still calls sdio_artosyn_remove() for that
same func later (module unload, or the daemon's own rmmod on shutdown)
-- which calls sdio_get_drvdata(func), gets back the already-freed dev,
and eventually double-frees it.

Confirmed on real hardware: kernel BUG at mm/slub.c (double free) inside
kfree(), called from sdio_artosyn_remove(), triggered by rmmod during a
normal `reboot`'s shutdown sequence -- a full kernel panic, not a
recoverable userspace error.

Fix: clear drvdata back to NULL in the fail path (matching what a
successful probe implicitly guarantees for remove() to find), and add a
defensive NULL check at the top of sdio_artosyn_remove() itself, since
dev being NULL there is now an expected, not exceptional, case -- covers
this specific path and any other way remove() could end up called for a
func this driver never finished binding to.

This is a pure safety net: neither change does anything on the normal
(probe succeeds) path, only on cleanup after a failure remove() would
otherwise have no safe way to handle.

diff --git a/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c b/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
--- a/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
+++ b/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
@@ -1462,6 +1462,20 @@ static int sdio_artosyn_probe(struct sdio_func *func,
     return 0;

 fail:
+    /* dev was already published via sdio_set_drvdata(func, dev) earlier in
+     * this function, and nothing clears it back to NULL on this path.
+     * Before the 0005 dnld-retry patch, oal_add_card() failing this way
+     * was effectively unreachable (a failed firmware chunk was silently
+     * treated as success instead), so this path was never really
+     * exercised. With that fix correctly propagating a real download
+     * failure, this became reachable, and the kernel still calls
+     * sdio_artosyn_remove() for func later (module unload, shutdown) --
+     * which calls sdio_get_drvdata(func) and gets back this already-freed
+     * dev, eventually double-freeing it. Confirmed live: kernel BUG at
+     * mm/slub.c (double free) in kfree(), called from
+     * sdio_artosyn_remove(), triggered by rmmod during shutdown. */
+    sdio_set_drvdata(func, NULL);
+
     sdio_claim_host(func);
     sdio_disable_func(func);
     sdio_release_irq(func);
@@ -1476,6 +1490,15 @@ static void sdio_artosyn_remove(struct sdio_func *func)
     int ret = -1;
     struct artosyn_sdio_device *dev = sdio_get_drvdata(func);

+    /* dev is NULL if probe() for this func failed before reaching the
+     * fail path's matching clear above, or in general for any func this
+     * driver never finished binding to -- nothing below is safe to run
+     * without a real dev. */
+    if (!dev) {
+        AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_MSG, "artosyn sdio remove: no drvdata for this func, nothing to do\n");
+        return;
+    }
+
     mutex_lock(&artosyn_mutex);
     removed = 1;
     mutex_unlock(&artosyn_mutex);
